  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mainly dry & seasonable weather continues through \nmid-week with gradually more sunshine. Lows mainly in the 30s-lower \n40s with highs generally well into the 50s.\n\nLow pressure will continue to lift to the north northwest across \neastern Canada through the middle of the week. This will result in a \npersistent westerly flow of air across the region. Outside a brief \nsprinkle/spot shower or two into early this evening...dry weather \nwill persist. In fact...we do expect to gradually see more diurnal \nsunshine Tue and especially by Wed as the upper level flow becomes \nflatter. Highs will be rather seasonable...mainly well into the 50s. \nLow temps will mainly be in the 30s to the lower 40s. Winds may gust \nbetween 15 and 25 mph at times...mainly during the daylight\nhours. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Monitoring potential storminess Friday into \nSaturday, with a still uncertain storm track and storm strength.\nMainly plain rain is favored for the lower elevations and \ncoastal plain. Rain possibly changing over to a period of wet \nsnow in the interior higher elevations, although wintry weather \nimpacts seem limited.\n\nWe continue to monitor developments regarding potential storminess \ncentered around late this week into early in the weekend. The last \n24-hours worth of guidance seems to be indicating a primary cyclone \ntransitioning to secondary low pressure development near the \nnorthern mid-Atlantic coast Thursday evening; varying storm \ntracks are still noted across the guidance pertaining to this \nsecondary low, tracking either slowly northeastward near the \nSouthern New England coast Friday and into early Saturday, or \npassing east well south of Nantucket through the same timeframe.\nThe latter storm track outcome seem to be more favored by the \nECMWF and some AI-trained solutions; the operational GFS and \nCanadian solutions are further northwest/closer to the coast, \nwhich would be a stronger, wetter and also colder solution and \nthe system's strength would allow for sub-freezing air aloft to \ndrain southward, supporting a possible transition to wet snow \nfor interior Southern New England. \n\nThere still is too much uncertainty across the guidance to really \nhone into a favored solution, and additional forecast adjustments \nare likely in the coming days. The lower elevations and coastal \nplain look to stay as predominantly rain given the influx of milder \nmaritime air. For the hilly terrain in interior Southern New \nEngland, latest ensemble snow probabilities show a moderate (20-50%) \nprobability of 1\" or more of snow confined to hilly terrain, and low \n(< 20%) probabilities of snow greater than 4 inches. Those also\nassume a 10:1 snow to liquid ratio; unless we get really heavy\nprecipitation rates which seems unlikely, those 10:1 SLR\naccumulations might not be valid given a wetter, gloppy and \npossibly lower-SLR snow character being more favored. It seems \nany wintry weather impacts in the terrain would be very limited \nto nil, and this is reflected by low probabilities of Minor \nwinter storm impacts in the Probabilistic Winter Storm Severity \nIndex graphic.\n\nStevens Institute guidance also shows a potential 1-1.5 ft storm \nsurge over the eastern coast Friday into Friday night; it seems \nunlikely we'll see coastal flooding, although Stevens Institute does \nshow minor coastal flooding in Nantucket with water levels around \n5.2 ft.   \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Cloudy, cool, and blustery weather for late \nweekend into early next week. \n\nWe become entrenched in a cool cyclonic flow aloft once the storm \nsystem exits offshore by Sunday. This supports a period of cooler, \ncloudy and blustery weather conditions Sunday into early next week, \nalthough dry weather seems favored. Temperatures may trend slightly \ncooler than normal as we open the early part of next week.",
